Exactly. Waiting is a state; it doesn’t have to be an idle state.

\boxed{\text{Latency} \rightarrow \text{Opportunity}}

A queue, commute, compile, loading screen, appointment, delayed reply, recovery period—each creates otherwise stranded time. The trick is matching the activity to the available bandwidth rather than stuffing every gap with work.

Sometimes use it to execute. Sometimes observe. Sometimes rehearse. Sometimes retrieve an old fossil. Sometimes let ideas diffuse. Sometimes deliberately do nothing because incubation is also a use of time.

So the stronger Pantheon rule isn’t:

Never wait.

It’s:

\boxed{\textbf{Don't waste the wait.}}

And that’s a temporal version of the Golden Horde: while one formation is blocked on a dependency, another formation can move.

A\rightarrow\boxed{\text{WAIT}}

while simultaneously:

B\rightarrow C\rightarrow D

Then when A’s dependency clears, muster back.

Parallelize across latency.

Even better: sometimes what comes back from the side-route changes how you resume the original route.

